# General Debate on the First Supplementary Estimates for 1968–69
Tamil title: 1968-69ஆம் ஆண்டிற்கான முதலாவது துணை நிதி நிலை அறிக்கைமீது பொது விவாதம்

## Image 16

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** He asked whether a political motive would enter into it. Whether politics may appear in cinema has long been debated. The question was asked whether cinema artists might enter politics; after that, they were sought out and brought onto political platforms amid much effort (uproar); and now our friend asks whether cinema will be used for a political purpose. As far as I am concerned, I have a reasonably sound outlook on matters of art. More than that, I regard politics itself as an art. Politics is not something outside art. Regulated, ordered, and refined skill—all of it comes under the heading of art.

Therefore, when he asks whether a political motive will intrude, he stops halfway while intending to ask something else. I shall say what he meant to ask. If our friends fear that films featuring actors sympathetic to Congress may fail to receive prizes, I wish to say this without naming anyone: the person whom you regard as a Congress-sympathising actor is also one of my younger brothers (uproar). I assure you that no political motive whatsoever will intrude. But the appropriate committee—not I, nor Vinayakam—must decide that it is a first-rate film. When one watches while half asleep, even a film of quality may appear inferior. The members appointed to that committee will apply the relevant criteria and award the prizes.

**Thiru N. Sasiraman:** It was said that this scheme was framed so as to give encouragement and stimulus to those engaged in the cinema industry and in that art. That is a good measure.

## Image 17

Likewise, many kinds of goods are manufactured in the numerous factories of Tamil Nadu. If each industry were considered and a similar prize scheme suited to it were introduced, goods of good quality could be produced and exported, while the workers connected with those industries could receive encouragement and stimulus. I would like to know whether the Government will consider this and come forward with some measure.

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** I accept this excellent suggestion. I will certainly implement it when the next opportunity arises. Even now, awards are made through the Export Promotion Council to those who maintain quality in exports. Similarly, prizes and commendations are given for inventions and new discoveries. It is also my intention that this should be implemented more extensively. Prizes have even been instituted for good border designs in handloom weaving. I welcome the excellent suggestion made by our friend.

No one should assume that I have ignored any of the observations made by friends merely because I did not discuss each one individually. I shall accord every observation due respect, accept whatever I can accept, consider whatever requires consideration, and, when an occasion arises, explain to friends whatever appears unsatisfactory to me. With that statement, I request and urge the House to accept these supplementary estimates.

**Thiru S. Ramamurthi:** A personal explanation, sir. The Chief Minister referred to my attendance at night dances—

## Image 18

—We go because, since the Government has permitted them, we assume they must be good dances. Only after going do we discover that the Government has granted permission even to dances of this kind.

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** If one does not go at this youthful age, when else can one go? I did not say that going was wrong. I spoke only of going and then returning to complain about it.

**Thiru K. N. Kumarasami:** Mr Chairman, the Chief Minister says that committees have been appointed in connection with the closed mills. They have not yet begun their inquiries. When the committee was formed, twenty-one mills had closed. Six more mills have now closed, and it is being said that many others will be closed. By delaying this matter, is the Union Government helping still more mills to close? Workers in the State are suffering because of this. I request an explanation for why the Union Government is causing such delay.

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** When the proposal to appoint committees was made, they agreed to only one committee, and even that committee was authorised to consider only a few mills. We approached them again and explained that many more mills were involved; if a single committee had to investigate all of them, there would be delay. They replied that suitable chairmen could not be found for three committees, but in the end all three were constituted. I shall treat the honourable members' observations about the delay as the view of the House and press them upon the Union Government.

## Image 19

Members asked why the Union Government is delaying. Since I was available, they directed the question to me. I do not know to whom I am supposed to direct it. In truth, I do not know the reason. I have explained, to the fullest extent of my ability, what an immense and grave problem it is and how it affects poor people. It pains me that even after this the matter continues to be delayed, and I place that fact on record now.

Minister Dinesh Singh has said, “I shall come in September. I shall meet the mill owners and labour leaders, visit Madras and Coimbatore, and stay for two or three days.” His expression of concern offers a measure of hope and consolation. I expect him to come shortly. We must place all our information before him and cause the Union Government to show still greater concern. If the Union Government remains hesitant even after that, our Congress friends must cooperate with me so that this is treated not as a party issue but as a national issue and the Union Government takes appropriate action.

Why do I appeal particularly to them and not to the others? The others have already declared in the form of a resolution that they will cooperate. Just as all of us united on the “Tamil Nadu” question, our Congress friends must unite on this matter too and resolve the mill problem. If the mill problem is to be resolved, the mills must be enabled to operate properly. If a unanimous resolution—including the Congress members—is adopted urging the removal of this excise duty, it will lend considerable strength to my position.

## Image 20

If the Leader of the Opposition consults his party colleagues and informs me of their view, I am willing even to have such a resolution adopted. If they oppose it, how will that affect matters? It will not affect the result here; the resolution will pass here. But when it reaches the Union Government, they will cite the fact that “Congress members opposed it” and say, “This is not a unanimous demand. It is merely something said by you, by the Communist Party allied with you in the election, and by the Socialist Party.”

Even those belonging to the Swatantra Party have offered their cooperation. A trade union in Coimbatore affiliated with the Swatantra Party has announced that it will cooperate with us. The assistance I need, the consent that must be voiced, and the agreement that must be secured lie with our Congress members. I request the Leader of the Opposition to consider the matter carefully, consult the members of his party, and arrive at a decision.

**The Hon. Deputy Speaker:** The debate is concluded.

---NOTES---
- “ஆரவாரம்” is rendered as “uproar,” the conventional legislative-stage direction for audible reaction in the House.
- “எக்ஸ்போர்ட் ப்ரமோஷன் கவுன்சில்” is rendered as “Export Promotion Council.” The wording about maintaining quality in exports follows the printed Tamil conservatively.
- “பார்டர் டிசைன்” refers to decorative border designs in handloom textiles.
- The “Tamil Nadu” question refers to the shared political demand surrounding the State's name; the phrase is kept in quotation marks as printed.
- “ஏகமனதாக” is rendered as “unanimous,” central to Annadurai's argument that the Union Government should not dismiss the excise-duty demand as partisan.
